# Part B — Why is this analysis problematic, and what does it lose?

The old method answers a useful, narrow question: **did the selected label change?** It cannot fully answer **how the interpretation changed**.

### B1. Ties make the chosen label depend on an extra rule

The next table repeats the *descriptive* change rate with the alphabetical order reversed, then shows the rate when both conditions have unique winners. Restricting to unique winners changes the sample, so that column is not a directly equivalent estimate. These are descriptive sensitivities; they do not establish robustness of every hypothesis test.
